PROMISES

Promise stands for work still running. These are host runtime's own promises,
so they behave like JavaScript promises: settles once, keeps result, awaiting
twice gives same answer, familiar combinators. Promise is a live value. Only
names for one: name REPL reported it under, load-mcp-1, or one you bound
yourself. Never its printed hash-and-angle-brackets form: reader refuses it.
- (await promise [timeout-ms]) wait, return value, re-raise its error. Waiting
  again on settled promise returns same value at once.
- (promise-all promises) one promise for every value, in order; rejects as soon
  as any does.
- (promise-all-settled promises) one promise for how each turned out, as
(:fulfilled value) or (:rejected "message") ; never rejects, so one failure
  keeps its siblings. Reach for this when loading several servers.
- (promise-any promises) first to succeed · (promise-race promises)
first to settle either way.
- (promise-state promise) gives :pending, :fulfilled or :rejected, without
  waiting.
- (promises) list what still runs · (cancel promise) abort work behind one.
GOTCHAS

- Hash-and-angle-brackets printout of a promise is a value that cannot be read
  back: typing one is always an error. Use name value was reported under.
  Awaiting that printed form is the common version; (await load-mcp-1) is what
  it meant.

RULES

- A PROMISE IS A LIVE VALUE, AND AN UNAWAITED LOAD IS DONE. Call returning a promise installs its result itself on settle. Step reporting load-mcp-1: a promise, still running … is COMPLETE: carry on, use what it loaded on a later step. Only await when you want its value in that same step; then wrap the single call: (await (load-mcp "acme")) . Never write a #<…> form — #<promise>, #<secret:KEY> — back into code: that text is a printout of a value reader cannot read back, so (await #<promise>) is an error. Refer to promise by name REPL reported ((await load-mcp-1)) or one you bound yourself.
